Cache macro expansions at their call sites, as Guile does ((*s7* 'cache-macro-expansions?))

s7 expands a macro call each time it is evaluated. With the new *s7*
field cache-macro-expansions? set to #t (it is #f by default, so stock
behavior is unchanged), a macro call evaluated again reuses its
expansion instead of calling the macro.

- The key is the argument list of the call, cdr of the call form, which
  is the same list each time the same code runs; the values of a weak
  eq? hash table are (macro expansion . made), and the entry is used only
  if the macro of the call is still that macro (redefined or shadowed
  macros are expanded again).
- Only op_macro_d caches: a macro applied as a function (by apply,
  for-each, sort!...) can be given a list which s7 reuses with other
  contents. The first call of some code goes through apply, which turns
  it into OP_MACRO_D, so the expansion is cached from the second call on.
- Not cached: bacros (expanded in the caller's environment), calls
  without arguments, expansions which are multiple values.
- The optimizer treats an expansion as new code, evaluated once, and can
  leave there information which holds only for that evaluation (a named
  let in the expansion of a loop macro then saw the variables of the
  first call). So made is the set of the pairs which the macro made (not
  those of its arguments, which are the caller's code, shared as
  before), and these pairs are copied each time the expansion is used:
  the evaluator gets new pairs, as from the macro.
- The table is emptied when it reaches 100000 entries.

s7test.scm gives the same results with the field #t and #f (and, with
#f, as without the patch, apart from the number of fields of *s7*).
In TeXmacs: the LaTeX export of the change log is 13-20% faster.

